description: Project Structure and code architecture — NestJS modules + Hexagonal folders + centralized tests
alwaysApply: false
---

### Project Structure Standards

#### 1) Modules per Domain Entity (NestJS default)
- Create **one NestJS module per bounded context**.
- Each module must have a `<entity>.module.ts` that wires providers and controllers.
- Cross-cutting code (shared types, utils, base classes) lives under `src/shared`.

#### 2) Hexagonal Architecture Folders (inside each module)
Use the following **folder layout per bounded context** (driving adapters under `presentation/`, not `infrastructure/`):

apps/backend/src/
└── <bounded-context>/
    ├── <bounded-context>.module.ts
    ├── domain/ # Pure domain (framework-agnostic)
    │   ├── entities/
    │   │   └── <entity>.entity.ts
    │   ├── value-objects/
    │   ├── errors/
    │   │   └── ...
    │   └── repositories/ # Repository interfaces (ports)
    │       └── ...
    ├── application/ # Use cases (framework-agnostic)
    │   ├── services/
    │   │   └── <entity>-query.service.ts
    │   └── use-cases/
    │       └── some-feature.use-case.ts
    ├── infrastructure/ # Driven adapters (external implementations)
    │   ├── clients/ # HTTP/gRPC/sdk client adapters
    │   │   └── ...
    │   └── persistence/
    │       ├── mappers/ # Mapping between database models to domain
    │       │   └── ...
    │       ├── schemas/
    │       │   └── <db-table>.db.ts
    │       └── repositories/
    │           └── postgresql/ # Repository implementations (adapters)
    └── presentation/ # Driving adapters (entrypoints)
        ├── mappers/
        │   └── <entity>-response.mapper.ts
        └── controllers/ # REST API controllers
            └── <entity>.controller.ts


**Rules:**
- **Domain** and **Application** must be **framework-agnostic** (no Nest imports/decorators). The exception is the `@Injectable()` annotation in the class.
- **Repositories** in `domain/repositories` define **interfaces**; concrete implementations go in `infrastructure/repositories`.
- External dependencies (DB, cache, auth, filesystem, 3rd-party APIs) are **adapters** under `infrastructure/` or `presentation/` (per direction).
- Driving adapters (e.g. HTTP controllers) belong to **`presentation/`**.
- Data access, external client code, and SDK wrappers belong to **`infrastructure/`**.
    + Do not leak SDK's APIs to domain
- **Adapters must implement explicit contracts defined under `ports/`**. Core code depends on the port interfaces; adapters provide concrete implementations wired via providers.
- **Request Flow Definition**:  
    + Requests arrive at an **endpoint** in a **controller** (`presentation/` layer).  
    + The controller calls a **method** in an **application service** or **use-case** (`application/` layer).  
    + The service calls a **port interface** implemented by an **adapter** or **repository** (`infrastructure/` layer) to access external resources (e.g., DB, cache, APIs).  
    + The adapter or repository interacts with the external dependency and returns domain objects or DTOs back up the chain.

#### 3) Centralized Tests and Colocated for unit test
All tests that are not 'unit' lives under `<root>/test` (not in `src/`). Organized by **type → module → subfolders** mirroring `src/modules/<bounded-context>`:

apps/backend/test/
├── integration/
│   └── <bounded-context>/
│       └── ...
└── e2e/
    └── <bounded-context>/
        └── ...

**Rules:**
- Test files must be placed in the **corresponding folder** under `test/<type>/modules/<entity>/...` that mirrors the path of the class/file under test.
- Recommended naming: `*.spec.ts` for unit/integration and `*.e2e-spec.ts` for e2e.

#### 4) Boundaries & Imports
- `presentation` may depend on `application` and DTOs, **never** on `infrastructure` directly.
- `application` may depend on `domain` and **ports** (interfaces), **never** on `infrastructure` or `presentation`.
- `infrastructure` implements ports and depends on `domain` types/ports and `application` DTOs (**only if necessary** for mapping).
- Enforce with dependency-cruiser by running `pnpm -F backend dep-check` to check it

#### 5) Providers & DI
- Use NestJS `@Injectable()` annotation to auto-wire dependencies into classes
- Use NestJS modules’ `providers` with `useClass`/`useFactory` to inject **ports → adapters** while keeping domain/application framework-agnostic.
- Use NestJS `useExisting` when binding a port to some adapter
- Export only the **ports** or **use-cases** needed by other modules; avoid leaking infrastructure details.

